WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:22.380
THE STATE OF AMAZON:&nbsp;

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:36.060
Amazon has become the world’s first company to&nbsp;
lose $1 trillion worth of market value dropping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:42.300
from $1.88 trillion down to $879 billion. This&nbsp;
isn’t just some sort of market over reaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:48.120
either as Amazon’s fundamentals aren’t looking&nbsp;
much better. In fact, Amazon’s trailing 12 months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:53.400
profit has plunged from over $30 billion&nbsp;
down to even negative numbers. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.920
they’ve been cutting jobs like crazy, having laid&nbsp;
off 27,000 people within the past several months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:05.340
itself. And all of this has unfolded within just 2&nbsp;
years of Jeff Bezos retiring from his position as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:11.580
CEO. This has led to many investors feeling that&nbsp;
it was Bezos that was the secret sauce to Amazon’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:17.040
success, sparking rumors that Bezos will return to&nbsp;
the helm to turnaround the company. One investor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.960
named Michael Batnink has even gone as far as&nbsp;
predicting that Bezos will return by the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:29.880
of 2023. This might sound unprecedented but that’s&nbsp;
not actually the case. In fact, it’s pretty common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.940
for CEOs to return if things aren’t going great.&nbsp;
Michael Dell for example retired back in 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.680
but as Dell started struggling as we headed&nbsp;
into the financial crisis, Michael got back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:47.340
to work. More recently, Bob Iger retired in 2020&nbsp;
after serving as Disney’s CEO for 15 years. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:53.040
after Disney stock fell 50% and Disney+ became a&nbsp;
money pit, Iger returned to his position in late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.640
2022. Likely the best example of a CEO returning&nbsp;
though is Howard Schultz who just can’t seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.620
get a break from Starbucks. Everytime he leaves&nbsp;
the company, the stock goes into the toilet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:11.340
so he basically just comes back on an as needed&nbsp;
basis having served 3 different terms so far. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.960
the idea of Bezos returning is not that far&nbsp;
fetched. And considering the current position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.600
of Amazon, it’s well warranted too. So, join me&nbsp;
as we look into whether Jeff Bezos will actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.340
make a grand reentrance and what exactly&nbsp;
that could mean for the future of Amazon.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:32.760
THE CASE FOR BEZOS:
Starting off with the case for Bezos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:38.100
the first thing we have to consider is whether&nbsp;
Bezos is even in a position to return and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:44.340
answer is absolutely. For one, he’s not even 60&nbsp;
years old meaning that he has plenty of working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:48.540
years left. Schultz and Iger were closer to&nbsp;
70 when they returned to their respective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:54.720
companies. Also, it’s not like he’s gotten super&nbsp;
involved in some sort of other effort. Yes, he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.880
been focusing on Blue Origin, his rocket company,&nbsp;
but it’s not like this is a new endeavor. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:06.720
often like to mock Bezos for copying Elon Musk and&nbsp;
SpaceX and in some aspects he definitely has. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.480
Blue Origin has actually been around since 2000.&nbsp;
So, most of its growth was achieved without Bezos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.940
being directly involved. In fact, Bezos isn’t&nbsp;
even the CEO of Blue Origin, it’s actually some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:24.600
guy named Bob Smith. Also, the only reason Blue&nbsp;
Origin is even possible is because of Bezos wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.300
from Amazon, so if Bezos wants to give Blue Origin&nbsp;
the best chance of success, it would make sense to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:36.060
protect his Amazon fortune which brings us into&nbsp;
the incentive argument. The bottom line is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:41.400
there is no one in the entire world that’s as&nbsp;
incentivized to turnaround Amazon as Bezos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.260
Amazon’s current CEO, Andy Jassy, owns about a&nbsp;
quarter billion dollars worth of Amazon stock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:52.920
Meanwhile, Bezos still owns about $123 billion&nbsp;
worth of Amazon stock. So, theoretically speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:58.320
Bezos should be 500 times as financially&nbsp;
incentivized as Jassy to turn around Amazon but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:04.320
I would argue that the motivation goes much deeper&nbsp;
than just financial gain. For Bezos, this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:10.080
company he founded way back in 1994 that he spent&nbsp;
virtually his entire adult life building. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.580
it’s safe to say that Bezos wouldn’t just sit&nbsp;
on the sidelines and watch his empire crumble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.680
especially if he could do something. In fact,&nbsp;
he’s probably the best person to do something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:25.080
because he’s already handled a far worse situation&nbsp;
back in early 2000s. During the dotcom crash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:32.160
Amazon stock straight up fell 95%. And at the&nbsp;
time, Amazon was still very much a startup during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.780
a time where virtually all other internet startups&nbsp;
were collapsing. This meant that it was extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:42.000
hard to raise money, recruit talent, and gather&nbsp;
the resources needed for a turnaround. But, Bezos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.760
was able to do it. He diversified the business&nbsp;
and launched AWS and the rest is history. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:54.120
Amazon is indeed doing bad, but they’re still one&nbsp;
of the biggest corporations on the planet with $64&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:59.760
billion in the bank. So, if Bezos was able to turn&nbsp;
around the 2000 Amazon, he has a pretty good shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:06.180
at turning around the 2023 Amazon. But, the truth&nbsp;
is that a lot of Bezos’ impact on Amazon wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:11.400
even be strictly strategic. This isn’t to say that&nbsp;
Bezos wouldn’t make great strategic decisions but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:16.260
I would argue that his bigger role at Amazon&nbsp;
would actually just be his presence itself. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:21.420
reality is that there’s only so much that a single&nbsp;
person can do. But, that single person’s spirit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:27.180
and morale can accomplish infinitely more. Now,&nbsp;
I know that the general public nor the media is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:33.600
exactly the biggest fan of Bezos. Most people just&nbsp;
see him as the bald slave driver. But, this isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:39.060
the case with Wall Street or Amazon’s corporate&nbsp;
side. Sure, they might not like him either but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:44.280
can assure you they think that he’s effective as&nbsp;
hell. I mean, just look at this. This is Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:50.160
under Bezos leadership. And this is Amazon under&nbsp;
Jassy’s leadership. If you were a corporate Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:55.080
employee and most of your compensation depended on&nbsp;
stock comp and growth, who would you rather have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.080 --> 00:06:00.060
lead the company? I’m gonna guess Bezos and&nbsp;
this is especially true for leadership who’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:05.100
entire compensation is basically stock. As such,&nbsp;
Bezos simply returning could be the morale boost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.420
that investors and executive leadership needs&nbsp;
to get back on track. If you’re not a fan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:14.460
Bezos returning though, don’t worry as there’s a&nbsp;
pretty strong case for him not returning as well.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:20.880
THE CASE AGAINST BEZOS:
For starters, the case against Bezos returning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.100
begins with the fact that he never really left in&nbsp;
the first place. Sure, he gave up his position as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:30.960
CEO but he simply switched to being executive&nbsp;
chairman. It’s not like Bill Gates who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:37.020
completely left Microsoft. Something else to note&nbsp;
is that even as CEO, Bezos was serving in more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:41.700
chairman type of capacity. He was never really in&nbsp;
the weeds of operations like Elon or something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:47.940
Bezos strongly felt that as a senior executive,&nbsp;
he job was to make a small number of super high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:53.940
quality decisions. In fact, he didn’t even start&nbsp;
work till 10 AM and his job each day was to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:59.340
make three good decisions. Now, I’m not here&nbsp;
to argue the effectiveness of this type of role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:05.640
but what I can say is that it’s not particularly&nbsp;
time intensive. Mentally intensive sure, but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:11.160
time intensive. So, it doesn’t really necessitate&nbsp;
Bezos to take on the CEO role again. He can just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:16.800
make his 3 decisions a day as executive chairman.&nbsp;
And speaking of decisions, Bezos strongly believes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.840
in making decisions that play out over the long&nbsp;
term. In fact, when friends congratulate him for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.000
a good quarter, he tells them “Thank you, but that&nbsp;
quarter was baked three years ago.” So, according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:33.000
to Bezos himself, a CEOs decisions can’t be judged&nbsp;
until 3 years later. News flash, Jassy hasn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:38.940
been CEO for 2 years so according to Bezos’ own&nbsp;
logic, none of this is Jassy’s fault. All of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:43.860
is actually Bezos’ fault as the decisions that led&nbsp;
to Amazon’s current situation where actually made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:49.980
in 2019 and 2020 when Bezos was CEO. So, while it&nbsp;
may seem like Jassy taking over is what screwed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:55.860
Amazon, the real culprit was actually Bezos last&nbsp;
decisions before he retired. So, by that logic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:01.680
it makes no sense for Bezos to takeover&nbsp;
once again as this was all his fault. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:07.020
Jassy hasn’t had enough time to really be judged&nbsp;
as CEO yet, so we can’t jump to conclusions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:12.480
him just yet. The reality is that Jassy is&nbsp;
actually super qualified to be CEO. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:18.180
that’s why he was picked for the job in the first&nbsp;
place. He joined the company way back in 1997&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:24.000
before the dotcom bust so he was also very much&nbsp;
involved in Amazon’s dotcom turnaround. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.100
you could argue that Jassy was the architect of&nbsp;
Amazon’s turnaround. Bezos original idea to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:33.180
around the company was to actually launch&nbsp;
something called Merchant.com. The service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:38.460
was supposed to help other retailers set up their&nbsp;
own e commerce platforms similar to Shopify. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:44.760
this turned out to be a massive failure and that’s&nbsp;
when Bezos allowed Jassy to startup AWS or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:51.000
cloud side of Amazon. And eversince then, AWS&nbsp;
has been the lifeblood of Amazon. In fact, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:56.400
the only reason that the retail business was even&nbsp;
viable as AWS was able pay for all the losses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.860
upfront investments. This is true even today as&nbsp;
Amazon’s retail busines is still teetering between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:07.800
profitability and unprofitabilty. So, really,&nbsp;
it was Jassy that turned around Amazon back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:13.740
in the 2000s. From that perspective, it actually&nbsp;
makes more sense to give Jassy a shot than Bezos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:18.360
a shot. It would also play out much better on&nbsp;
the media which brings us into our next point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:24.660
which is that Bezos is a PR nightmare. It’s no&nbsp;
secret that the media hates Bezos and there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:29.820
plenty of good reasons to hold a grudge against&nbsp;
the guy. The reality is that his involvement or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:34.560
lack of involvement doesn’t really change&nbsp;
any of the bad attributes of Amazon. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:39.120
not like the lives of warehouse and delivery&nbsp;
workers suddenly became magical after Bezos’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:44.280
departure. It’s also not like Amazon suddenly&nbsp;
stopped avoiding taxes as much as possible. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:49.320
when Bezos is not directly involved, all of this&nbsp;
is just written off as a big evil corproation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.060
doing big evil corporation things. When Bezos&nbsp;
is involved, however, it all of a sudden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:59.940
becomes personal. It’s no longer Amazon that’s&nbsp;
causing all this damage, it’s actually Bezos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:04.380
the world’s richest man, who’s doing all this.&nbsp;
He’s the reason. He’s the one abusing warehouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:09.600
workers. He’s the one evading taxes. He's the&nbsp;
physical manifestation of evil rich dude. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:15.180
this is accurate or not is irrelevant. When the&nbsp;
public has such a large bias against a dude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:20.040
probably not the best idea to put him back in&nbsp;
the spotlight especially as a consumer business.

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:27.000
HINTS FROM BEZOS:
Clearly, there’s quite few reasons going for and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:32.820
against Bezos, but what does Bezos think? Well,&nbsp;
he hasn’t publicly talked about the situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:38.580
but that doesn’t really mean anything. This isn’t&nbsp;
the type of situation that you plan on. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:43.980
there’s a reason that he retired. Bob Iger for&nbsp;
example says that he was very very surprised by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:50.460
his own return to Disney. So, Bezos hasn’t exactly&nbsp;
dropped any hints but we can dubiously speculate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:55.260
based on his principles and overall thoughts.&nbsp;
While Bezos is likely disappointed by the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:00.960
state of Amazon, I don’t think he’s particularly&nbsp;
surprised. In fact, he’s been anticipating this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.580
moment for his entire career as he always&nbsp;
knew that Amazon’s downfall was inevitable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:37.380
With that in mind, Bezos could return to Amazon&nbsp;
but what’s the purpose. Sure, he might be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:43.200
to turnaround the company and get them to $2, 3,&nbsp;
5 trillion, but what does that accomplish. He’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:48.420
just be even more hated and it’s not like saved&nbsp;
Amazon completely. He would’ve simply delayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:54.600
their downfall by another 10 or 20 years but the&nbsp;
eventual death of Amazon is still inevitable. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.600 --> 00:12:00.000
why waste time on such a vanity metric especially&nbsp;
when he could be working on rockets or his earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.340
fund and hopefully actually be creating long term&nbsp;
change. This is precisely what Bill Gates did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:11.580
After he left in 2000, Microsoft got destroyed. In&nbsp;
fact, the stock didn’t move for the next 17 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:17.460
But, Gates didn’t come back. He actually stepped&nbsp;
back further, sold more stock, and focused even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:22.500
more on his foundation. And given that Bezos has&nbsp;
also reduced his stake down to the single digits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:28.620
I’m pretty sure that Bezos leans on the side of&nbsp;
not returning. But who knows, Amazon is his baby.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:34.140
So, I’m sure he’ll have tough time just watching&nbsp;
the carnage from the sidelines. It’s not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:39.180
Amazon that’s facing an existential crisis. All&nbsp;
of big tech is going through similar turmoil. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:42.720
you’d like to learn why all monopolies&nbsp;
eventually fall, check out this video.

